    "available_at": "2026-11-10T20:50:31.749385+00:00",
    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Triumph TF-E Bikes Get Even More Enduro With Updates for the 2027 Model Year\nCyberbeast Is the Only Cybertruck Variant Still Available To Order, but the Price Will Shock You\n66 photos\nPhoto: Tesla\nDays after Musk confirmed the Cybertruck would be killed by mid-2027, Tesla scrapped all trims but the Cyberbeast. Not content with the price hike it operated a few days ago, Tesla raised the price of the Cyberbeast again, this time to $109,990. If you count the price of FSD and other extras, the Cyberbeast is now even more expensive than the Foundation Series equivalent in 2023-2024.\nThe Cybertruck's demise could have been a simple story of a product being launched, selling poorly, and being retired. The sales numbers have been disappointing, to put it mildly, and Tesla could never justify the high production costs for such a low-production vehicle. However, when you zoom out, you notice that the Cybertruck is a part of a trend that points to Tesla winding down its automotive operations.\nThe Cybertruck's cancellation comes just months after the Model S and Model X ended their careers with a Signature Edition limited series. The two Tesla flagship models were sacrificed to make way for the Optimus robot production line. The Cybertruck fell victim to the Cybercab, or so Musk wants us to believe. Both are bogus reasons, as neither Optimus nor the Cybercab require more production space.\nWhen Tesla announced that the Model S and Model X would disappear, interest in the two EVs spiked, despite repeated price hikes. The Signature Edition models sold out despite the outrageous $159,420 price . Apparently, Musk believes that this recipe might also work for the Cybertruck.\nWhile there's no confirmed \"farewell edition\" Cybertruck, its price has been cranked up to Foundation Series levels. At the same time, cheaper variants have been discontinued days after their prices have also been increased. This leaves the Cyberbeast as the only option for those who are still crazy enough to buy a Cybertruck for $109,990.\nFor the price, there's no FSD package paid upfront ($12,000 until April 2024 and $8,000 afterward), no lifetime Premium Connectivity package, and none of the accessories that came with the Foundation Series Cybertruck. This makes the current Cyberbeast even more expensive than the special edition model. The original did not sell very well, and it's doubtful that it will be any different now.\nThe fact that Tesla no longer accepts reservations for the AWD and Premium AWD variants appears to confirm my concerns. It is highly unlikely that Tesla will ever deliver the trucks to those who ordered the base model for $59,990 in February. Some reservation holders came to the same conclusion and upgraded their orders to the Premium AWD model for a $20,000 difference. However, the Premium AWD variant has also been discarded.\nThe Cybertruck has been a disappointment from the day delivery started. The price was twice as high as what Tesla announced in 2019, and the range was a far cry from the 500 miles promised. On top of that, Elon Musk made some questionable political choices, and the Cybertruck was regarded as a symbol of his transformation. Many people avoided buying one or even being seen in one, with catastrophic effects on sales.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nTesla Cybertruck Tesla Elon Musk EV eol\nAbout the author: Cristian Agatie\nAfter his childhood dream of becoming a \"tractor operator\" didn't pan out, Cristian turned to journalism, first in print and later moving to online media. His top interests are electric vehicles and new energy solutions. Full profile\nUP NEXT Ford Analyzed Model Year 2021 Mustang Mach-E Batteries, Most Retain More Than 90% Battery Health",
